  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Humidity briefly trends down tonight, but then rises \nagain Monday afternoon. Above normal temperatures continue through \nTuesday.\n\nExpecting mainly rain-free conditions for this portion of the \nforecast. The exception should be Monday afternoon into the evening, \nwhen a round of diurnal showers and thunderstorms will be possible.\n\nHumidity drops later tonight behind a weak cold front. This front \nshould not go far, and return as a warm front Monday afternoon into \nthe evening. Dew points increase again just to the south of this\nfront. A passing cold pool across northern New England should\nhelp to generate CAPE values of 750-1,250 J/kg by late Monday\nafternoon. The greatest risk for any thunderstorms likely\nremains west of our region.\n\nOnce again, this warm front moves back south as a cold front\nMonday night into Tuesday. Not looking humid enough to keep a\nmention of showers in the forecast, especially that early in \nthe day when this front should pass by. Humidity starts to fall\nonce more Monday night, but really get into the comfortable\nrange Tuesday afternoon and night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warmer and less humid Wednesday, with a strong \ncold front moving in early on Thursday bringing a risk for\nshowers and a low risk for storms.\n\nSummer warmth continues into Wednesday, but with a substantial \ndecrease in humidity levels (e.g. aftn dewpoints in the upper \n50s to mid 60s) with westerly non-convergent flow offering dry \nweather. Partly sunny with highs in the mid to upper 80s and \nlows in the lower to mid 60s. \n\nA rather strong mid/upper trough and surface cold front moves\nthrough Southern New England on Thursday. This frontal system\nushers in a true substantial change in airmass. Ordinarily, that \nwould point to an active day, but the timing is the biggest\nmitigating factor, as the front looks to reach the eastern MA\ncoast by early afternoon per loose global model consensus. Thus \ncurrent expectation is for minimal to weak diurnal instability \nand an attendant low risk for thunderstorms, but if the front \nends up slower than current thinking then the risk for storms \nwould increase to go along with the risk for strong storms. \nPartly to mostly cloudy skies with highs in the lower 80s, with \ndewpoints falling through the afternoon and evening into low-mid\n50s post- frontal, and lows in the 50s to around 60. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Nice stretch of later-summer weather Friday \ninto the weekend.\n\nMain story for Friday into the weekend is a much cooler and\ndrier airmass that entrenches itself over the Northeast. Cool\nadvection brings 850 mb temps down to around +8 to +10C,\nsupportive of highs in the 70s to lower 80s with mostly \nsunny/partly cloudy skies and dewpoints falling into the 40s. \nOverall a nice stretch of late- summer weather if slightly \ncooler than normal for mid August. Next chance for showers or \nstorms could arrive by late Sunday or Monday but the timing is \nstill a question mark.",
